## What changed in MapLibre GL JS v4?

`maplibregl.addProtocol()` used to accept a **callback-style** handler:

```js
// v3 (and earlier)
maplibregl.addProtocol('my', (params, callback) => {
  fetch(params.url)
    .then(r => r.arrayBuffer())
    .then(buf => callback(null, buf))
    .catch(err => callback(err));
});
```

Starting with **v4** the API became **promise-based** and the handler now receives an **`AbortController`** instead of a callback.  
The handler **must resolve to an object that contains a `data` property** (the raw tile bytes).  
If you return a bare `ArrayBuffer`, `Blob`, `Uint8Array`, or `Response`, MapLibre will treat the tile as “no data” and you’ll see blank tiles (or a silent failure).

```js
// v4+ signature
maplibregl.addProtocol('my', async (params, abortController) => {
  // …fetch tile…
  // return { data: <ArrayBuffer|Uint8Array|Blob|Response>, cacheControl?, expires? }
});
```

Because of that change, any code that still uses the old callback signature will stop working after the upgrade.

---

## Quick fix for a custom tile protocol

### 1️⃣ Replace the callback with an async function

```js
// BEFORE (v3)
maplibregl.addProtocol('my', (params, callback) => {
  fetch(params.url)
    .then(r => r.arrayBuffer())
    .then(buf => callback(null, buf))
    .catch(err => callback(err));
});
```

```js
// AFTER (v4+)
maplibregl.addProtocol('my', async (params, abortController) => {
  // Abort the request if the map cancels it (e.g. user pans away)
  const response = await fetch(params.url, { signal: abortController.signal });

  if (!response.ok) {
    // Throwing aborts the tile request and makes MapLibre show an error tile
    throw new Error(`Tile fetch failed: ${response.status} ${response.statusText}`);
  }

  // You can return any of the following as `data`:
  //   - ArrayBuffer
  //   - Uint8Array
  //   - Blob
  //   - Response (the whole fetch response)
  const buffer = await response.arrayBuffer();

  // The contract requires the wrapper object:
  return { data: buffer };
});
```

**Key points**

| What you must do | Why |
|------------------|-----|
| **Return `{ data: … }`** | MapLibre extracts the tile bytes from the `data` field. |
| **Accept `abortController`** | Allows the request to be cancelled when the map no longer needs the tile (e.g., fast pan/zoom). |
| **Throw on non-OK responses** | Propagates the error to MapLibre so it can show a “missing tile” placeholder. |
| **Do not call a callback** | The callback API was removed; calling it does nothing and may even cause errors. |

### 2️⃣ Optional: expose cache-control / expires

If you want the browser or a CDN to respect HTTP-style caching for individual tiles, you can add the optional fields:

```js
return {
  data: buffer,
  cacheControl: response.headers.get('Cache-Control'), // e.g. "public, max-age=31536000"
  expires: response.headers.get('Expires') // optional
};
```

MapLibre will forward those values to the browser’s cache layer.

### 3️⃣ Register the protocol **once**, before any map is created

```js
import * as maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';

// Register at app start (e.g. top-level module, React root effect, etc.)
maplibregl.addProtocol('my', async (params, abortController) => {
  const response = await fetch(params.url, { signal: abortController.signal });
  if (!response.ok) throw new Error(`Tile fetch error ${response.status}`);
  const buf = await response.arrayBuffer();
  return { data: buf };
});
```

If you later need to clean up (e.g., in a single-page app that completely unloads the map), you can remove it:

```js
maplibregl.removeProtocol('my');
```

---

## Using the **pmtiles** library – you don’t have to rewrite anything

If you are serving PMTiles (the most common use-case), the `pmtiles` npm package already ships a **v3-compatible shim** that works with both the old callback API and the new promise API.

```bash
npm install pmtiles
```

```js
import * as pmtiles from 'pmtiles';
import * as maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';

// The shim detects whether the second argument is an AbortController
// and adapts accordingly, so the same one-liner works on v3, v4, v5, v6.
const protocol = new pmtiles.Protocol();
maplibregl.addProtocol('pmtiles', protocol.tile);
```

> **Why this works:** `protocol.tile` checks the type of the second argument.  
> * If it’s a callback (v3) it uses the old style.  
> * If it’s an `AbortController` (v4+) it returns a promise that resolves to `{data,…}`.  

So, for PMTiles you can keep the exact same line you had before the upgrade.  
Just make sure you are using **pmtiles ≥ 3.0.0**; older 2.x releases still use the callback-only signature and must be upgraded.

---

## Full example – a custom vector source that uses a PMTiles file

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre GL JS v4 + PMTiles</title>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>#map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }</style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script type="module">
  import * as maplibregl from 'https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js';
  import * as pmtiles from 'https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/pmtiles@3.0.0/dist/index.min.js';

  // Register the PMTiles protocol (works on v3-v6)
  const protocol = new pmtiles.Protocol();
  maplibregl.addProtocol('pmtiles', protocol.tile);

  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      sources: {
        // Note: use `url:` (not `tiles:`) so the protocol can read the header
        // and supply the correct minzoom / maxzoom.
        world: {
          type: 'vector',
          url: 'pmtiles://https://my-bucket.s3.amazonaws.com/world.pmtiles'
        }
      },
      layers: [
        {
          id: 'water',
          type: 'fill',
          source: 'world',
          'source-layer': 'water',
          paint: { 'fill-color': '#a0c8f0' }
        },
        {
          id: 'roads',
          type: 'line',
          source: 'world',
          'source-layer': 'road',
          paint: { 'line-color': '#777', 'line-width': 1 }
        }
      ]
    },
    center: [0, 0],
    zoom: 2
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

*The only line that matters for the upgrade is the `addProtocol` call – the rest of the style stays the same.*

